item.text:
Provisional severity class: severe authoritarian closure and coercive entrenchment. No score.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation, State Duma Elections, 19 September 2021: Needs Assessment Mission Report","publisher":"OSCE Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights","publication_date":"2021-06-25","url":"https://www.osce.org/files/f/documents/0/f/491066_0.pdf","claim":"The OSCE mission said approved constitutional changes enabled the incumbent president to run for two more terms, extended presidential powers over judges, and recent legal changes further limited standing, assemblies, foreign agents, media, and internet regulation; interlocutors said the amendments were tailored to limit competition.","locator":"PDF pp. 2-4, lines 45-160","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct OSCE election-observation reporting with specific legal and political findings from contemporaneous interlocutors.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline entering 2022; directly relevant to the 2022 political-freedom environment.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Putin’s presidency and the broader Kremlin-controlled electoral framework; the report directly names the incumbent president benefit and executive/legal changes.","contrary_evidence":["State institutions told ODIHR the framework would ensure genuine competition and respect the rule of law."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.4","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No OSCE observers for Russian parliamentary elections following major limitations","publisher":"OSCE Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights","publication_date":"2021-08-04","url":"https://odihr.osce.org/odihr/elections/russia/494488","claim":"The OSCE said Russian authorities imposed limitations that prevented an independent observer mission, severely restricting the number of observers and undermining effective and credible election observation.","locator":"Press release paragraphs 1-5","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct OSCE statement about host-state restrictions on independent observation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline entering 2022; relevant to the electoral environment that carried into 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Russian authorities under Putin, which controlled the observation access and election conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The authorities cited sanitary-epidemiological concerns as the reason for the restrictions."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.4","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin approves changes allowing him to stay in power until 2036 By Reuters","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2020-03-10","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/russias-ruling-party-backs-idea-to-allow-putin-to-run-again-for-president-ria-2105790","claim":"Reuters reported that Putin opened the door to constitutional changes that would let him remain in power until 2036 by resetting his presidential term tally to zero.","locator":"Lines 420-430","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a public parliamentary speech and constitutional trajectory, but hosted on a syndication platform.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline shaping 2022 authority and succession expectations.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin because he personally backed the constitutional reset in parliament.","contrary_evidence":["Putin said he favored term limits once Russia became politically mature and framed the move as a stability issue."],"lenses":["4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Doc. 15827: compendium of written amendments (Final version)","publisher":"Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe","publication_date":"2023-10-12","url":"https://pace.coe.int/en/files/33020/compendium","claim":"PACE said the abolition of presidential term limits for Putin violated constitutional and international principles and that the concentration of presidential power, together with weak checks and balances, had turned Russia into a de facto dictatorship.","locator":"Lines 179-190, 203-204","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official parliamentary text summarizing constitutional and democratic concerns, though adopted after the target year.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2020-2022 constitutional trajectory relevant to 2022 governance.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Putin’s regime and the incumbent-benefiting constitutional changes.","contrary_evidence":["This is a later Assembly text, not a contemporaneous 2022 finding."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation: Authorities impose censorship on media and crush peaceful anti-war protests following invasion of Ukraine","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/eur46/5272/2022/en/","claim":"Roskomnadzor ordered media to rely only on official Russian sources for invasion coverage and threatened sanctions for spreading false information, effectively imposing censorship.","locator":"Opening summary and first paragraph","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights organization statement with specific legal and regulatory details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin’s state apparatus because Roskomnadzor acted as the official media watchdog under his government.","contrary_evidence":["Russian authorities framed the measure as preventing false information about the war."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Kremlin’s ruthless crackdown stifles independent journalism and anti-war movement","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/03/russia-kremlins-ruthless-crackdown-stifles-independent-journalism-and-anti-war-movement/","claim":"Amnesty said Russian authorities blocked independent media, banned Facebook and Twitter, and had arbitrarily arrested more than 13,800 people at anti-war rallies since 24 February 2022.","locator":"Bulleted summary and opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous NGO reporting with concrete arrest and censorship figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin’s government because the crackdown was implemented by state authorities and Roskomnadzor/police.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities described the war and restrictions as necessary responses to the invasion and misinformation."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Arbitrary Detentions of Anti-War Protesters","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-02-26","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/02/26/russia-arbitrary-detentions-anti-war-protesters","claim":"HRW said Russian authorities arbitrarily detained at least 5,982 anti-war protesters across Russia between February 24 and 28 and threatened media outlets with blocking if their reporting differed from the official narrative.","locator":"Update paragraph with OVD-Info figures and opening summary","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous HRW reporting that cites an independent monitor and describes state conduct directly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Russian authorities under Putin through police and media-regulator actions.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities warned that unsanctioned protests and support for foreign organizations could be prosecuted as security threats."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Brutal Arrests and Torture, Ill-Treatment of Anti-War Protesters","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-09","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/09/russia-brutal-arrests-and-torture-ill-treatment-anti-war-protesters","claim":"HRW reported that police detained protesters brutally and that the ban on public gatherings remained in force, showing coercive suppression of peaceful protest after the invasion.","locator":"Update lines around March 13 and opening summary","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights reporting with specific protest-detention examples and an official legal context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Putin’s government because police and public-order rules were used to suppress dissent.","contrary_evidence":["Russian officials treated the protests as unsanctioned and maintained COVID/public-order restrictions."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russians dare to protest war; AP dares to report it","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2022-04-22","url":"https://www.ap.org/news-highlights/best-of-the-week/2022/russia-crackdown-against-war-protesters/","claim":"AP reported that Russia’s draconian fake-news law made fair, accurate, critical reporting almost impossible and that protesters were being charged for tiny anti-war signs and slogans.","locator":"Paragraphs 99-106","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"AP’s reporting is firsthand, specific, and contemporaneous, though it is a secondary media source.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Putin government because the prosecutions and chilling effect flowed from the new legal regime and police enforcement.","contrary_evidence":["The state portrayed the law as necessary to stop false claims about the military and war."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Repressions in Russia in 2022","publisher":"OVD-Info","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://ovd.info/en/repressions-russia-2022","claim":"OVD-Info said Russia began internal repression on an unprecedented scale in 2022, with more than 21,000 arrests, at least 370 criminal defendants for anti-war speech, more than 200,000 blocked internet resources, and 11 treason sentences.","locator":"Opening summary and lines 59-63","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major independent Russian monitoring project with specific year-end counts and direct reporting methodology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Russian state authorities under Putin because the figures describe arrests, prosecutions, and blocking carried out by the state.","contrary_evidence":["The report includes a broad range of protests beyond anti-war actions and is an advocacy-monitoring product."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Репрессии в России в 2022 году","publisher":"ОВД-Инфо","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://data.ovd.info/repressii-v-rossii-v-2022-godu","claim":"OVD-Info reported 19,478 anti-war detentions since February 24, the use of conscription notices at mobilization protests, preventive detentions via facial recognition, and repeated police violence against protesters.","locator":"Lines 69-87","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Local-language annual monitor with concrete year-specific counts and detailed mechanisms of coercion.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Russian state authorities under Putin because the practices are described as police and security-service conduct in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes other non-anti-war protests, which shows the repression was broader than one issue area."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Authorities close down Amnesty International’s Moscow Office","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-04-08","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/04/russia-authorities-close-down-amnesty-internationals-moscow-office/","claim":"Russian authorities shut down Amnesty International’s Moscow office and also closed or delisted other international NGOs, while blocking Amnesty’s Russian-language website.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and background section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous NGO statement about direct state action against civil society organizations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin’s government through the Ministry of Justice and media regulator actions.","contrary_evidence":["The ministry said the offices had legal violations."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.4","4B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian journalists are being silenced to stifle reporting of protests","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-11-24","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/11/russia-journalists-and-independent-monitors-being-silenced-to-stifle-reporting-of-protests-new-report/","claim":"Amnesty said Russian authorities used a sophisticated system of restrictions and severe reprisals to crush public protests and suppress reporting by journalists and independent monitors.","locator":"Opening summary","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting tied to a focused new report on protest monitoring and reprisals.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Putin government because the report describes state reprisals and law-enforcement conduct.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities justified restrictions as law enforcement and public-order measures."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Human Rights Committee Considers Report of the Russian Federation in the Absence of a Delegation, Experts Raise Issues on the Persecution of Journalists and the Arrests of Protesters","publisher":"United Nations Office at Geneva","publication_date":"2022-10-20","url":"https://www.ungeneva.org/en/news-media/meeting-summary/2022/10/human-rights-committee-considers-report-russian-federation","claim":"UN experts said there were thousands of cases of harassment and persecution of journalists, hundreds of Russian journalists had been detained for reporting on the war or protests, and there had been mass arrests in response to peaceful demonstrations.","locator":"Lines 99-108 and 163-167","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN meeting summary of an official treaty-body review with multiple expert interventions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Russian state authorities under Putin because the committee described state detention and harassment of journalists and protesters.","contrary_evidence":["The Russian Federation did not send a delegation, so the review could not include an on-the-record state rebuttal."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"We publish a report on political prisoners and political repression in Russia in 2022","publisher":"Political Prisoners. Memorial","publication_date":"2023-08-04","url":"https://memopzk.org/en/analytics/we-publish-a-report-on-political-prisoners-and-political-repression-in-russia-in-2022-2/","claim":"Memorial said repression of public dissent reached unprecedented levels in 2022, political opposition was effectively outlawed, independent media were shut down, censorship was institutionalized, protest activity was suppressed, and the number of political prisoners reached 513 by the end of 2022.","locator":"Lines 55-73","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major Russian rights project summarizing its own monitored casework and year-end counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin’s regime because the report describes state repression, censorship, and police-force conduct in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["This is an advocacy-monitoring report and is retrospective rather than contemporaneous."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Политзаключённые и политические репрессии в России в 2022 году","publisher":"Поддержка политзаключённых. Мемориал","publication_date":"2023-08-04","url":"https://memopzk.org/analytics/politzaklyuchyonnye-i-politicheskie-repressii-v-rossii-v-2022-godu/","claim":"Memorial said 2022 was marked by de facto military censorship, 74 new names were added to the main political-prisoner list, and more than 5,000 administrative cases and 200-plus criminal cases targeted anti-army speech.","locator":"Lines 2450-2452","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Local-language retrospective report with explicit annual statistics and repression categories.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin’s regime because the report describes state repression in response to anti-war speech and public dissent.","contrary_evidence":["The report is a retrospective summary and not a contemporaneous government record."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin signs law expanding Russia's rules against 'LGBT propaganda'","publisher":"Euronews","publication_date":"2022-12-06","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/12/06/russia-rights-lgbt","claim":"Reuters-reported coverage said Putin signed a law expanding Russia’s LGBT propaganda restrictions so broadly that it effectively outlawed any public expression of LGBT behaviour or lifestyle and could penalize positive or neutral information online and in media.","locator":"Lines 659-666","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-based secondary reporting on a signed law and its stated scope.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin because he signed the law and it was enacted by his state.","contrary_evidence":["The Kremlin framed the law as protecting traditional values."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.7","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2026-07-21","url":"https://rsf.org/en/country/russia","claim":"RSF says that since Russia’s large-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022, almost all independent media have been banned, blocked, or declared foreign agents or undesirable organizations, and that all others are subject to military censorship.","locator":"Lines 396-396","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"RSF country page is current but explicitly summarizes the post-2022 media environment in Russia.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective summary of the 2022 media regime carried into the chapter period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Putin’s government and wartime censorship apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["This is a current summary page rather than a 2022-dated report."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Russian Federation","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-12-14","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/russian-federation","claim":"HRW said that in 2022 Russian authorities launched an all-out drive to eradicate public dissent, introduced war-censorship laws with long prison sentences, and expanded the foreign-agents regime while suppressing political speech.","locator":"Lines 78-115","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual country chapter summarizing multiple 2022 mechanisms of repression and censorship.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin’s government because the laws and prosecutions were enacted and enforced by the state.","contrary_evidence":["The chapter also notes that some repression trends predated 2022, so not all deterioration is unique to that year."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Data on politically motivated criminal persecutions in Russia","publisher":"OVD-Info","publication_date":"not available","url":"https://ovd.info/en/pulse/criminal","claim":"OVD-Info states that it monitors politically motivated criminal cases daily, compiles a public database, and sources cases from court websites, Russian media, direct testimony, relatives, lawyers, and other rights groups.","locator":"Lines 61-80","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Methodology page describing the monitoring system behind the statistics used elsewhere in the dossier.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Cross-cutting source-family reliability for the 2022 repression evidence.","ruler_attribution":"This does not directly attribute conduct to Putin; it supports the reliability of the OVD-Info evidence family.","contrary_evidence":["This is methodological rather than conduct evidence."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Elections in Russian Federation","publisher":"OSCE Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights","publication_date":"not available","url":"https://odihr.osce.org/odihr/elections/russia","claim":"The OSCE elections portal shows that Russia’s election-observation record includes only limited and heavily constrained missions after 2021, underscoring the narrow international scrutiny available around the chapter period.","locator":"Page listing of Russia election missions and reports","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Metadata page rather than a substantive report, but useful for mission history and coverage context.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline and observation-coverage context for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Putin-era election environment, but only as background context.","contrary_evidence":["The page itself is a mission index, not a findings document."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.4","4B.10"]}

Search ledger:
- Candidates found: about 40
- Documents opened: 20 promising docs
- Duplicates / near-duplicates: about 11
- Irrelevant / out-of-period: about 5
- Inaccessible / partial: about 4
- Accepted distinct URLs: 20

Material gaps:
- Direct primary Russian legal texts would sharpen attribution for the March 2022 fake-news/discrediting laws, the later foreign-agent expansion, and the December LGBT law.
- A direct 2022 official or court record on candidate exclusions, voter access, and observer constraints would strengthen the election-manipulation side of 4B.1/4B.2/4B.8.
- The record is already strong on censorship, protest suppression, political-prisoner growth, and anti-minority restriction; weaker on any contemporaneous evidence of ruler self-binding, institutional strengthening, or genuine tolerance for opposition victory.
- A better local-language court record on Memorial liquidation and a few flagship journalist/protester prosecutions would improve ruler attribution and legal precision.

Next best query directions:
1. Search Russian primary legal sources for the 4 March 2022 criminal-code amendments and the July/December 2022 “foreign agents” and LGBT laws.
2. Search Russian court and CEC materials for 2022 candidate-registration, observer, and appeal decisions.
3. Pull a direct court or ministry record on the liquidation of Memorial and other NGOs, plus one or two named journalist or protest cases from 2022.
